<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Volhynia</h1><p class="st-meta">1340 CE – 1452 CE (approx.) · capital Lutsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Lithuanian-ruled duchy of Volhynia, held by Lubart and later Svidrigaila from the great castle at Lutsk.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia</a> collapsed after 1340, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubart">Lubart</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a> who had married into the local dynasty, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>. For decades he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> for the whole inheritance of Galicia-Volhynia, holding Volhynia while Poland kept <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubart">Lubart</a> built the brick castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> that still bears his name, and his rule mixed Lithuanian princes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Ruthenian nobles and church. The duchy had its own court and was one of the most valuable lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Svidrigaila</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a>, ruled it from the 1430s after losing the struggle for the grand ducal throne.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Svidrigaila</a> died in 1452, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> lost its status as a separate duchy and became a voivodeship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. In 1569 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> transferred it to the Polish Crown.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-volhynia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Volhynia" width="320" height="317"><figcaption>Image: Kpalion · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galicia-Volhynia_map.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1340 CE – 1452 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lutsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lubart, Svidrigaila</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ruthenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lithuanian Volhynia, Grand Duchy of Volhynia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1396&amp;focus=duchy-of-volhynia" aria-label="Duchy of Volhynia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5233 414 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Volhynia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5553 670L5571 720M5553 670L5556 562"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5571 720h0M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5553 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5553 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1396&amp;focus=duchy-of-volhynia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
